          <w lemma="συνεργός">συνεργῷ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">ἡμῶν</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="2">
        <text>and to Apphia our sister, and to Archippus our fellow soldier, and to the assembly in your house:</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="Ἀπφία">Ἀπφίᾳ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῇ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀδελφή">ἀδελφῇ</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="Ἄρχιππος">Ἀρχίππῳ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῷ</w>
          <w lemma="συστρατιώτης">συστρατιώτῃ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">ἡμῶν</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῇ</w>
          <w lemma="κατά">κατ’</w>
          <w lemma="οἶκος">οἶκόν</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σου</w>
          <w lemma="ἐκκλησία">ἐκκλησίᾳ·</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="assembly" kind="departure" lemma="ἐκκλησία" traditional="church">The Greek word was the ordinary term for a civic gathering and the Septuagint’s word for Israel assembled; “church” now suggests a building or an institution. Here it is a group that meets in a private home. “Church” is the near-universal rendering and readers know what it refers to; “assembly” is used throughout this translation so that the one Greek word is recognizable everywhere.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="3">
        <text>Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="χάρις">χάρις</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">ὑμῖν</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="εἰρήνη">εἰρήνη</w>
