          <w lemma="ὁ">τῶν</w>
          <w lemma="προσευχή">προσευχῶν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">μου,</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="5">
        <text>because I hear of your love and the faith that you have toward the Lord Jesus and for all the holy ones.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="ἀκούω">ἀκούων</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σου</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὴν</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγάπη">ἀγάπην</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὴν</w>
          <w lemma="πίστις">πίστιν</w>
          <w lemma="ὅς">ἣν</w>
          <w lemma="ἔχω">ἔχεις</w>
          <w lemma="πρός">πρὸς</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὸν</w>
          <w lemma="κύριος">κύριον</w>
          <w lemma="Ἰησοῦς">Ἰησοῦν</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="εἰς">εἰς</w>
          <w lemma="πᾶς">πάντας</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοὺς</w>
          <w lemma="ἅγιος">ἁγίους,</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="holy ones" kind="departure" lemma="ἅγιος" traditional="saints">The word is used of all members of the community as people set apart for God, echoing the Septuagint’s language for Israel. “Saints” now suggests canonized individuals or people of exceptional virtue. Many translations keep “saints”; others use “God’s people”.</note>
        <note term="your love and the faith that you have toward the Lord Jesus and for all the holy ones" kind="ambiguity" lemma="" traditional="">The translation keeps the Greek word order. Many interpreters read it as a crossed pattern, with faith directed toward the Lord Jesus and love directed toward the holy ones, and some translations rearrange the sentence to say so. Others take both love and faith (or faithfulness) as directed to both.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="6">
        <text>I pray that the partnership of your faith may become effective in the knowledge of every good thing that is in us for Christ.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="ὅπως">ὅπως</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">ἡ</w>
          <w lemma="κοινωνία">κοινωνία</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῆς</w>
          <w lemma="πίστις">πίστεώς</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σου</w>
          <w lemma="ἐνεργής">ἐνεργὴς</w>
          <w lemma="γίνομαι">γένηται</w>
          <w lemma="ἐν">ἐν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐπίγνωσις">ἐπιγνώσει</w>
          <w lemma="πᾶς">παντὸς</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγαθός">ἀγαθοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐν">ἐν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">ἡμῖν</w>
          <w lemma="εἰς">εἰς</w>
          <w lemma="Χριστός">Χριστόν·</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="the partnership of your faith" kind="ambiguity" lemma="κοινωνία" traditional="the fellowship of your faith; the sharing of your faith">This verse is widely regarded as obscure. The Greek noun means having something in common, and here may mean Philemon’s partnership with others that springs from his faith, his participation in the faith, or his generosity; “partnership” is chosen because Paul appeals to Philemon as a “partner” in verse 17. It does not mean telling others about one’s faith. The closing words, literally “in us into Christ”, are also uncertain and may mean “for Christ’s sake” or “leading us toward Christ”.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="7">
        <text>For I have had much joy and encouragement from your love, because the hearts of the holy ones have been refreshed through you, brother.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="χαρά">χαρὰν</w>
          <w lemma="γάρ">γὰρ</w>
          <w lemma="πολύς">πολλὴν</w>
          <w lemma="ἔχω">ἔσχον</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="παράκλησις">παράκλησιν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐπί">ἐπὶ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῇ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγάπη">ἀγάπῃ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σου,</w>
          <w lemma="ὅτι">ὅτι</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὰ</w>
          <w lemma="σπλάγχνον">σπλάγχνα</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῶν</w>
          <w lemma="ἅγιος">ἁγίων</w>
          <w lemma="ἀναπαύω">ἀναπέπαυται</w>
          <w lemma="διά">διὰ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοῦ,</w>
          <w lemma="ἀδελφός">ἀδελφέ.</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="hearts" kind="background" lemma="σπλάγχνον" traditional="bowels (KJV); hearts">The Greek word literally means the inner organs, regarded as the seat of the deepest feelings. Paul uses it three times in this short letter (verses 7, 12 and 20): Philemon has refreshed the hearts of others, Onesimus is Paul’s own heart, and Paul asks Philemon to refresh his heart.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="8">
        <text><p/>So although I have great boldness in Christ to command you to do what is proper,</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="διό">Διό,</w>
          <w lemma="πολύς">πολλὴν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐν">ἐν</w>
          <w lemma="Χριστός">Χριστῷ</w>
          <w lemma="παρρησία">παρρησίαν</w>
          <w lemma="ἔχω">ἔχων</w>
          <w lemma="ἐπιτάσσω">ἐπιτάσσειν</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοι</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὸ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀνήκω">ἀνῆκον,</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="9">
        <text>I appeal to you instead on the basis of love—being who I am: Paul, an old man and now also a prisoner of Christ Jesus—</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="διά">διὰ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὴν</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγάπη">ἀγάπην</w>
          <w lemma="μᾶλλον">μᾶλλον</w>
          <w lemma="παρακαλέω">παρακαλῶ,</w>
          <w lemma="τοιοῦτος">τοιοῦτος</w>
          <w lemma="εἰμί">ὢν</w>
          <w lemma="ὡς">ὡς</w>
          <w lemma="Παῦλος">Παῦλος</w>
          <w lemma="πρεσβύτης">πρεσβύτης</w>
          <w lemma="νυνί">νυνὶ</w>
          <w lemma="δέ">δὲ</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="δέσμιος">δέσμιος</w>
          <w lemma="Χριστός">Χριστοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="Ἰησοῦς">Ἰησοῦ—</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="an old man" kind="ambiguity" lemma="πρεσβύτης" traditional="the aged; an old man">The Greek word normally means “old man”, and most translations render it so. A nearly identical word means “ambassador”, the two were sometimes confused in spelling, and some scholars think Paul means “an ambassador and now also a prisoner of Christ Jesus”. “Old man” fits an appeal that sets aside authority in favor of love.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="10">
        <text>I appeal to you for my child, whose father I became in my chains—Onesimus,</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="παρακαλέω">παρακαλῶ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σε</w>
          <w lemma="περί">περὶ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐμός">ἐμοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="τέκνον">τέκνου,</w>
          <w lemma="ὅς">ὃν</w>
          <w lemma="γεννάω">ἐγέννησα</w>
          <w lemma="ἐν">ἐν</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοῖς</w>
          <w lemma="δεσμός">δεσμοῖς</w>
          <w lemma="Ὀνήσιμος">Ὀνήσιμον,</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="Onesimus" kind="background" lemma="Ὀνήσιμος" traditional="">Onesimus was a slave in Philemon’s household (verse 16) who had come to faith through Paul during Paul’s imprisonment. He is usually thought to have run away, though the letter never says how he came to be with Paul. His name, common for slaves, means “useful”, and Paul plays on it in verse 11 (“useless … useful”) and again in verse 20, where “benefit” translates a verb from the same root as the name.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="11">
        <text>who was once useless to you but is now useful both to you and to me.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τόν</w>
          <w lemma="ποτέ">ποτέ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοι</w>
          <w lemma="ἄχρηστος">ἄχρηστον</w>
          <w lemma="νυνί">νυνὶ</w>
          <w lemma="δέ">δὲ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοὶ</w>
          <w lemma="καί">καὶ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">ἐμοὶ</w>
          <w lemma="εὔχρηστος">εὔχρηστον,</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="12">
        <text>I am sending him back to you—him, that is, my own heart.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="ὅς">ὃν</w>
          <w lemma="ἀναπέμπω">ἀνέπεμψά</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοι</w>
          <w lemma="αὐτός">αὐτόν,</w>
          <w lemma="οὗτος">τοῦτ’</w>
          <w lemma="εἰμί">ἔστιν</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὰ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐμός">ἐμὰ</w>
          <w lemma="σπλάγχνον">σπλάγχνα·</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="13">
        <text>I would have liked to keep him with me, so that on your behalf he might serve me in my chains for the good news,</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="ὅς">ὃν</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">ἐγὼ</w>
          <w lemma="βούλομαι">ἐβουλόμην</w>
          <w lemma="πρός">πρὸς</w>
          <w lemma="ἐμαυτοῦ">ἐμαυτὸν</w>
          <w lemma="κατέχω">κατέχειν,</w>
          <w lemma="ἵνα">ἵνα</w>
          <w lemma="ὑπέρ">ὑπὲρ</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐγώ">μοι</w>
          <w lemma="διακονέω">διακονῇ</w>
          <w lemma="ἐν">ἐν</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοῖς</w>
          <w lemma="δεσμός">δεσμοῖς</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τοῦ</w>
          <w lemma="εὐαγγέλιον">εὐαγγελίου,</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="good news" kind="departure" lemma="εὐαγγέλιον" traditional="gospel">The Greek was an ordinary word for a public announcement of good news, also used of imperial proclamations. “Gospel” has become a religious term or a book title, so “good news” is used throughout this translation. Many modern translations alternate between the two.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="14">
        <text>but I did not want to do anything without your consent, so that your good deed would not be forced but freely chosen.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="χωρίς">χωρὶς</w>
          <w lemma="δέ">δὲ</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τῆς</w>
          <w lemma="σός">σῆς</w>
          <w lemma="γνώμη">γνώμης</w>
          <w lemma="οὐδείς">οὐδὲν</w>
          <w lemma="θέλω">ἠθέλησα</w>
          <w lemma="ποιέω">ποιῆσαι,</w>
          <w lemma="ἵνα">ἵνα</w>
          <w lemma="μή">μὴ</w>
          <w lemma="ὡς">ὡς</w>
          <w lemma="κατά">κατὰ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀνάγκη">ἀνάγκην</w>
          <w lemma="ὁ">τὸ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγαθός">ἀγαθόν</w>
          <w lemma="σύ">σου</w>
          <w lemma="εἰμί">ᾖ</w>
          <w lemma="ἀλλά">ἀλλὰ</w>
          <w lemma="κατά">κατὰ</w>
          <w lemma="ἑκούσιος">ἑκούσιον·</w>
        </greek>
      </verse>
      <verse num="15">
        <text>For perhaps this is why he was separated from you for a while: so that you might have him back forever,</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="τάχα">τάχα</w>
          <w lemma="γάρ">γὰρ</w>
          <w lemma="διά">διὰ</w>
          <w lemma="οὗτος">τοῦτο</w>
          <w lemma="χωρίζω">ἐχωρίσθη</w>
          <w lemma="πρός">πρὸς</w>
          <w lemma="ὥρα">ὥραν</w>
          <w lemma="ἵνα">ἵνα</w>
          <w lemma="αἰώνιος">αἰώνιον</w>
          <w lemma="αὐτός">αὐτὸν</w>
          <w lemma="ἀπέχω">ἀπέχῃς,</w>
        </greek>
        <note term="forever" kind="register-exception" lemma="αἰώνιος" traditional="for ever">This is the adjective elsewhere rendered “eternal”, used here in an adverbial way that English cannot reproduce with the adjective; “forever” gives the same sense. It contrasts with “for a while” (literally “for an hour”). The word can also carry the overtone of the age to come: the new bond between the two as brothers outlasts this life.</note>
        <note term="he was separated from you" kind="ambiguity" lemma="χωρίζω" traditional="he departed">The verb is passive, and “from you” is supplied for English. Paul avoids saying that Onesimus left or ran away, and many interpreters see in the passive a hint that God’s purpose was at work in the separation, which the cautious “perhaps” supports. It can also be read simply as “he was parted from you”, with no agent implied.</note>
      </verse>
      <verse num="16">
        <text>no longer as a slave but more than a slave, a beloved brother—especially to me, but how much more to you, both in the flesh and in the Lord.</text>
        <greek>
          <w lemma="οὐκέτι">οὐκέτι</w>
          <w lemma="ὡς">ὡς</w>
          <w lemma="δοῦλος">δοῦλον</w>
          <w lemma="ἀλλά">ἀλλὰ</w>
          <w lemma="ὑπέρ">ὑπὲρ</w>
          <w lemma="δοῦλος">δοῦλον,</w>
          <w lemma="ἀδελφός">ἀδελφὸν</w>
          <w lemma="ἀγαπητός">ἀγαπητόν,</w>
